- Store field: `crdtSubs map[subKey]map[uint64]*crdtSubscription` guarded by pollMu.
- `AddCRDTSubscription(documentID, deviceID string) (wakes <-chan struct{}, revoked <-chan struct{}, initial CRDTState, hasInitial bool, pending []CRDTState, unregister func())` — under pollMu: if closed → wake signaled, no initial read? Hmm — if store is closed, the DB handle may still be open (InterruptWaits doesn't close db; Close closes db after subWG.Wait). For the closed case, follow existing pattern: signal wake immediately, unregister no-op, and don't read state (hasInitial=false). Actually wait — during shutdown, InterruptWaits is called, then handlers drain. A new subscription during drain... readiness gate still passes; the handler would register, get immediate wake, then check Closing() → 1001 close. Initial state read while closed: db still open (Close happens after drain), so reading is safe. But to keep it simple and match existing semantics, on closed: return immediately-signaled wake, no initial state, empty pending, no-op unregister. The server loop will see Closing and end with 1001. Hmm, but the server sends initial state first before checking closing? Order in my serve loop: register → send initial → sweep pending → loop {park/flush}. If closed, we should end with 1001 promptly. I'll have the server check s.Closing() before sending initial. Actually simpler: server checks Closing first thing after registration. Let me structure serveCRDTSubscription:

```go
wakes, revoked, initial, hasInitial, pending, unregister := s.AddCRDTSubscription(...)
defer unregister + NetClose
start read pump (clientGone)
if s.Closing() → endSubscription(1001); return
if hasInitial → WriteText(frame(initial))
for _, st := range pending → WriteText(frame(st))
lastValue := latest sent value (initial or last pending) — for dedupe
loop:
